{
  "about": [
    "The text parity corpus. Every case is drawn three times -- by DreamGUI on the gallery stage, by Slate through FWidgetRenderer, and by Chrome headless (Tools/TextParity/Make-ChromeReference.ps1) -- and DreamGUI.RHI.TextParity.<id> compares the three. README.md in Tools/TextParity says how the numbers are taken.",
    "A case: id; text (UTF-8; characters that cannot be seen or that combine with the one before are written as \\u escapes, everything else literally); font (a key of the fonts table); size (font size in pixels); width (the box width in pixels, 0 for no wrapping: the box is then the canvas less the padding); lineHeight ('normal', or a percentage of the font's own line height, the way DreamGUI's LineHeightPercentage and Slate's line height scale it); letterSpacing (pixels); rich (DreamGUI rich-text markup, <lang=xx> included); lang; align (start, end, center, left, right, justify); textJustify and textAlignLast (CSS's, for a justified case: auto, inter-word, inter-character, none; auto, start, center, end, justify); dir (ltr, rtl, auto); wrap ('anywhere' breaks a word that does not fit a line, 'normal' lets it overflow); overflow ('', 'ellipsis', 'middleEllipsis', or 'clamp' with maxLines); transform ('', 'uppercase'); tabSize (CSS tab-size in spaces); outline ({width in em, color}); shadow ({x and y in em, +y down, color}: a drop shadow without blur); fill (a CSS gradient the face is filled with, below); canvas ([width, height] in CSS pixels); scale (device pixels per CSS pixel); smallTextRaster ('off' draws DreamGUI's text from the distance field at every size); reference ('chrome', or 'slate' for what Chrome cannot draw); targets; flags; variants.",
    "Defaults: width 0, lineHeight normal, letterSpacing 0, rich false, lang en, align start, textJustify auto, textAlignLast auto, dir ltr, wrap anywhere, tabSize 8 (which Chrome and DreamGUI use; a case that states one is n/a for Slate, which has no tab size), no fill, scale 1, reference chrome, canvas as below. The text is black on white at (padding, padding) of the canvas.",
    "scale: DreamGUI draws on a stage of canvas times scale pixels with its root laid out at the canvas's size (the canvas scale), Slate at that DPI scale, Chrome with --force-device-scale-factor. Pictures are canvas times scale pixels, every position is in canvas pixels. Give a canvas the scale takes to whole pixels.",
    "flags: 'breaks' -- DreamGUI's line starts must equal the reference's; 'corners' -- a mask difference is counted; 'reportOnly' -- known not to match yet: neither the breaks nor the targets are asserted, only reported.",
    "targets: limits on DreamGUI against the reference, asserted unless the case is reportOnly -- inkMass (relative, either way), inkBounds (pixels, every edge of the ink's bounds), caretMax (pixels), baseline and pitch (pixels, either way), edgeWidth (pixels wider than the reference's edges, rows and columns), greyFraction (relative, either way), strokeEdge (edge width per stroke: pixels wider than the reference's, rows and columns), inkLinear (ink in linear light, relative, either way), greyVsSlate (the grey fraction against Slate's rather than the reference's, relative, either way; dark on light only). An object of them, or the name of one in targetSets. Small text is held to strokeEdge, inkLinear and greyVsSlate (smallText; smallTextCjk leaves the grey out, and so does Size_14, whose grey is 8 % more than Slate's on the pictures from before the multisampled target took the sRGB flag, and which that fix puts about on the 10 % line: reported until a run after the fix has been looked at); edgeWidth, greyFraction and inkMass, the measures before them, are still reported for every case.",
    "fill: a CSS gradient the text's face is filled with -- linear-, radial- and conic-gradient and their repeating- forms, with the angles, positions, shapes, sizes, 'in srgb | srgb-linear | oklab' and colours FDreamGradient::ParseCss reads. DreamGUI paints it as the text's FacePaint, measured across the text as a block; Chrome's page as background-image with background-clip: text and color: transparent on the paragraph, whose box is the text block. A rich case paints a run instead, with <gradient=css-written-without-spaces>, measured across the run on both sides. Write angles rather than 'to <corner>' (CSS turns a corner to the actual box's, DreamGUI to the square's) and hex colours rather than names (CSS's green is #008000, a rich text's <color=green> #00FF00). Slate has no gradient fill: n/a. Every fill case is also drawn as a solid mask in the ink colour on both sides, nothing painted, outlined or shadowed (DreamGUI with the paint off, Chrome's <case>.mask.png); the painted pictures are compared on the pixels both masks cover fully, and the numbers are reported only.",
    "reference 'slate': the case is measured against Slate and has no Chrome reference (Chrome has no middle ellipsis). Where Slate has no equivalent of a case -- justification, a stated tab size, fallback faces chosen by the text's language -- Slate does not draw it: n/a.",
    "wordBreak: the CSS word-break Chrome's page uses (normal by default). The Korean cases use keep-all: DreamGUI and Slate break Korean between words (eojeol), as Korean pages ask Chrome to with keep-all; Chrome's own default breaks between any two syllables.",
    "LongWord_Url is reportOnly: Blink's line breaker never breaks after '/' before a letter, where UAX #14 (which DreamGUI, Slate and Firefox follow) allows it, so the two differ on every path segment by design.",
    "variants: 'narrow' adds <id>_1px, the same case in a box 1 pixel wide with wrap normal, so that every break opportunity becomes a line break; its canvas is max(512, 2 * padding + 3 * size) wide and min(4096, 2 * padding + (UTF-16 length + 1) * ceil(1.6 * size)) tall. 'inverse' adds <id>_Inverse, white on black. 'field' adds <id>_Field, the same picture asked of DreamGUI's distance field (smallTextRaster off): small text drawn both ways against one reference, always reportOnly.",
    "fonts: each key names its faces in fallback order, the primary first; a face is its file, or {file, lang, unicodeRange, scale} for a fallback meant for some languages (semicolon-separated cultures), some code points (CSS unicode-range) or drawn at a scale (CSS size-adjust). bold, italic and boldItalic are the primary's true style faces where it has them. $(EngineDir) is the engine's Engine directory, $(WindowsFonts) the system's font directory. kind 'bitmap' asks DreamGUI for a bitmap font instead of a distance field. optional true: the key names a file not every machine has, and its cases are skipped where one is missing.",
    "Emoji: every engine draws the engine's own Noto Color Emoji (CBDT bitmaps) through @font-face, a composite sub-font and a fallback entry. EmojiSegoe is Windows' Segoe UI Emoji, held to carets and metrics only: Chrome paints its COLRv1 gradients, FreeType (DreamGUI and Slate) its COLRv0 layers. Emoji_TextPresentation asks for text presentation the Emoji key has no monochrome face for, so each engine shows what it does then."
  ],
